{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-17 08:26:56 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-17 15:26:56 +0700\n\nReviewed the complete revision against HEAD `6a9d77fc1e9c877af42cb7b2e6fcc82b2fea8207`; tracked files match HEAD. No files edited. Verification used specification and Zend-source inspection; PHP CLI fixtures were not executed.\n\nImmediate argument acquisition, uninitialized typed-reference handling and the blocking meta-rule close their round-6 findings. Iterator migration and readonly cloning still contain claimed-row defects:\n\n1. **[P1] Row 54 incorrectly discards iterator-copy tracking.**  \n   [Row 54](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792224000000-architect-ts-php-r7.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:51) says `zend_array_dup` does not carry iterators. Zend explicitly duplicates iterator positions and links those copies so migration can preserve the current position:\n   ```php\n   $a = [10, 20, 30]; $n = 0;\n   foreach ($a as &$v) {\n       echo \"$v,\";\n       if ($n++ === 0) { $b = $a; $a[2] = 99; }\n   }\n   ```\n   Zend continues with `20,99`; the proposed migration resets to the copied table’s internal pointer and visits `10` again. Preserve iterator-copy lineage and consult it before falling back to unrelated-table migration. The ordinary assignment-to-unrelated-table fix is correct. [Zend duplication and iterator tracking](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_hash.c).\n\n2. **[P1] Row 53 rejects ordinary classes without constructors.**  \n   [Row 53](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792224000000-architect-ts-php-r7.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:16) makes a missing constructor an error. `class C {}; new C();` is valid PHP. Zend creates the object and, when no constructor exists, either skips the call or uses a dummy call to consume arguments.\n\n   Separate “no constructor” from inaccessible constructors, abstract classes and other resolution failures. Test both zero arguments and a side-effecting argument on a class without a constructor. [Zend NEW handler](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_vm_def.h).\n\n3. **[P1] Row 57 delays named-argument errors past later side effects.**  \n   [Row 57](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792224000000-architect-ts-php-r7.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:21) explicitly waits until entry after evaluating all arguments. For an ordinary resolved user function:\n   ```php\n   function f($x) {}\n   function side() { echo \"late\"; return 1; }\n   f(bad: 1, x: side());\n   ```\n   Zend rejects `bad` during its send; `side()` does not run. This is not an internal-versus-user-function distinction. Apply name resolution and duplicate detection at the relevant send operation, with pending-call cleanup on failure. [Zend named-argument validation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_execute.c), [SEND handlers](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_vm_def.h).\n\n4. **[P1] Readonly clone handling confuses reinitialization with initialization after unset.**  \n   [Row 52](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792224000000-architect-ts-php-r7.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:65) treats every second modification as an error. This valid sequence must succeed:\n   ```php\n   class C {\n       public readonly int $x;\n       function __construct() { $this->x = 1; }\n       function __clone() { unset($this->x); $this->x = 2; }\n   }\n   ```\n   Unset consumes the reinitialization allowance but leaves the property uninitialized. Assignment then follows ordinary readonly **initialization** rules from the permitted scope. Track initialized state separately from the reinitialization flag; distinguish two overwrites from unset followed by initialization. [Zend property write/unset handlers](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_object_handlers.c).\n\nThe tracked-corner mechanism and revised landing rule are acceptable. These findings concern normative rows within the claimed fragment, rather than demands to implement deferred corners. Oracle confirmation before implementation landing remains necessary, but does not resolve contradictions already present in the design.\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES"}}
